mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# Monitoreg Backend API\n\n## Purpose\n\nBackend for the Monitoreg smart vertical-garden app. It stores the Bluetooth link state to an Arduino UNO (HC-05/HC-06), sensor measurements, irrigation configuration and events, registered plants, and alerts. The canister is the durable record; the actual Bluetooth radio link lives in the browser, which relays serial frames to and from this canister.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\n- The app uses Internet Identity. The frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n- Registration gates access. A direct API caller must register once by calling `_initialize_access_control` as a signed-in (non-anonymous) caller before any role-guarded call, including guarded queries. The first caller to initialize becomes `#admin`; every later caller becomes `#user`.\n- An unregistered or anonymous caller receives a trap on guarded endpoints. `AccessControl.getUserRole` traps with `User is not registered` for a signed-in principal that never registered, and returns `#guest` for an anonymous caller. Guarded methods then trap with messages such as `Unauthorized: Only users can register plants`.\n- A caller can be unregistered while assuming the app already knows it: registration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend, so a principal that never did so is unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered.\n- Bluetooth link methods (`setPairedDevice`, `disconnectBluetooth`, `reportConnectionLost`, `getConnectionStatus`, `getPairedDevice`, `parseSerialFrame`) are unguarded and callable by any caller.\n- Plant, measurement, irrigation and alert methods require a registered `#user` or `#admin`.\n\n## Units and encodings\n\n- Timestamps are `Int` nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, as returned by `Time.now()`.\n- `soilMoisture` and `ambientHumidity` are percentages (`Nat`, 0-100). `temperature` is degrees Celsius (`Int`).\n- `moistureThreshold` and `moistureNeed` are soil-moisture percentages. `maxDurationSeconds` is seconds.\n- Optional sensor fields (`?Nat`, `?Int`) are `null` when the sensor is absent or did not report. Presence flags (`#present` / `#absent`) record whether the sensor reported in that frame.\n- `photoFileHash` is an opaque object-storage file hash (`?Text`), not a URL.\n- `Actuator` is `#none`, `#pump` or `#valve`; pump and valve are mutually exclusive.\n\n## Serial protocol\n\nCommands sent to the Arduino are one line each, terminated by `\\n`: `READ`, `PUMP:1`/`PUMP:0`, `VALVE:1`/`VALVE:0`, `STOP`, `THRESH:<n>`, `MAXDUR:<n>`. Frames received are `SOIL:<n>,TEMP:<n>,HUM:<n>` with any field optional. `parseSerialFrame` validates a frame and returns `#ok(SensorFrame)` or `#malformed(Text)`.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- `setPairedDevice` marks the link `#connected` and records the device. `disconnectBluetooth` marks it `#disconnected`. `reportConnectionLost` marks it `#lost`.\n- `recordMeasurement` stores a frame, updates the last-seen time, and raises a `#sensorFailure` alert when the soil sensor is absent.\n- `getLatestMeasurement` returns the most recent measurement or `null`. `getPlantStatus` derives `#healthy`, `#needsWater` or `#needsAttention` from the latest reading and the plant's `moistureNeed`.\n- `getHistory(limit)` returns up to `limit` moisture/temperature points, most recent first.\n- Poll `getConnectionStatus` and `getLatestMeasurement` to refresh the UI; there is no push channel.\n\n## Retry safety\n\n- Read methods (`getConnectionStatus`, `getPairedDevice`, `parseSerialFrame`, `getLatestMeasurement`, `getPlantStatus`, `getIrrigationConfig`, `getActiveActuator`, `listIrrigationEvents`, `listPlants`, `getPlant`, `listAlerts`, `getHistory`) are idempotent queries.\n- `setPairedDevice`, `disconnectBluetooth`, `reportConnectionLost`, `setIrrigationConfig`, `startManualIrrigation`, `stopIrrigation`, `emergencyStopIrrigation`, `addPlant`, `updatePlant`, `deletePlant` and `acknowledgeAlert` are updates. `addPlant` and `recordMeasurement` allocate a new id on every call, so a retried call creates a duplicate row. `updatePlant`, `deletePlant`, `acknowledgeAlert`, `setIrrigationConfig` and the Bluetooth setters are idempotent.\n- `startManualIrrigation` records an event each call; a retry while irrigation is already active records a `#blocked` event.\n\n## Errors and limits\n\n- Guarded methods trap with `Unauthorized: ...` when the caller lacks the required role.\n- `startManualIrrigation` is blocked when an emergency stop is latched, when an actuator is already active, or when `#none` is requested; the block is recorded as a `#blocked` event.\n- `emergencyStopIrrigation` latches the emergency stop and halts any active irrigation.\n- Irrigation is stopped with outcome `#connectionLost` when the Bluetooth link is lost, and with `#maxDurationReached` when it exceeds `maxDurationSeconds`."
  };
};
